== Lignin Dissolution
The lignin dissolution solution was made using #qty(50, "g") sodium hydroxide and #qty(25, "g")  sodium sulfite per trial. In addition, a hotplate with a stirring bar, multiple two-liter beakers, glass thermometers, and a fume hood were required. Other general laboratory equipment was also used. About #qty(80, "g") citric acid was used per trial to neutralize the solution. The wood supply consisted of 11 blocks of pine.
\ \
== Compression & Densification
The main component of wood compression was a 10-ton hydraulic press with a pressure gauge. This press consisted primarily of an inverted car jack. Also used was a wood containment apparatus constructed of multiple blocks of cut and welded steel. Polyamide heating pads were attached around the steel containment block to heat the setup to over #qty(100, "celsius"). Two #qty(24, "V") DC power supplies were connected to the polyamide heating pads. Ceramic insulation was wrapped around the apparatus like a box to keep heat trapped inside. 
\ \
== Strength Testing
The hydraulic press was again required for this segment. Three other steel blocks not connected to any additional segments were used. A smartphone video camera and video analysis software were used to record pressure readings. In addition, a Tinius-Olsen impact testing machine was used through the York College of Pennsylvania.

#pagebreak()

== Lignin Dissolution
Each trial, #qty(50, "g") of sodium hydroxide and #qty(25.2, "g") of sodium sulfite were dissolved in #qty(500, "mL") distilled water, and the solution was heated to and monitored at #qty(100, "celsius"). A block of wood was held submerged in solution with a beaker stand, clamp, and stirring rod, and maintained like this for 6 hours.
 \ \
== Compression & Densification
The six original steel blocks were welded into a containment apparatus. Its walls were wrapped with the heating pads, wired two to each power supply. Each trial, the apparatus was put in the hydraulic press with a block inside. The applied pressure was increased to between #qtyrange(3, 7, "MPa"), depending on the trial. This setup was left under pressure in a ceramic insulation foam box for 24 hours. 
 \ \
== Strength Testing
Each block was cut along the grain into five equal segments. Two steel blocks were aligned in the press #qty(70, "mm") apart. Each segment was placed onto the blocks to have a #qty(70, "mm") unsupported span. A thinner steel block was placed onto the wood, used as the contact point as the pressure was slowly increased until fracture. A video taken of the press gauge was used to determine maximum load.

An additional block of wood was cut into $#qty(10, "mm") times #qty(10, "mm") times #qty(55, "mm")$ segments and tested impact strength using a Tinius-Olsen Impact tester using the low-impact Charpy test per the machine's standard operation. 